//! Wi-Fi and Radio tabs of the "Interface <name>" window (G1.30g): explanations.
pub(super) const HELP: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("iface_wifi.addresses", "Addresses on this port now. **(DHCP)**: given by a DHCP client; it may change when the lease renews."),
    ("iface_wifi.antennas", "Antennas (chains) the card sends and receives with: 2×2 carries two streams at once, which doubles the top rate. The real rate also depends on the access point."),
    ("iface_wifi.ap", "The BSSID: the MAC address of the access point radio the port is on. On a network with several access points (mesh, roaming) it changes when the port moves."),
    ("iface_wifi.band", "The frequency band and the standard negotiated with the access point. 2.4 GHz reaches further, with 3 free channels and much interference; 5 GHz is faster and cleaner, with less reach. Wi-Fi 6 is 802.11ax, Wi-Fi 5 is ac, Wi-Fi 4 is n."),
    ("iface_wifi.bands", "What the radio does in each band: standards, widest channel, how many channels the country in force allows (DFS: listen for radar before sending, ETSI EN 301 893) and the most power allowed."),
    ("iface_wifi.beacons", "Access point beacons that should have arrived and did not (an AP sends ~10 a second). Growing: weak signal, interference or the AP too far; the card disconnects after many in a row."),
    ("iface_wifi.chains", "The signal on each receive antenna. A large difference (over ~10 dB) usually means a loose antenna, a bad antenna cable or the card placed badly."),
    ("iface_wifi.channel", "The channel in use, the primary channel's frequency and the width. Wide channels (80, 160 MHz) join several 20 MHz ones: faster, more exposed to interference. The **centre** is the middle of the whole channel."),
    ("iface_wifi.country", "The country the router is in: it decides which channels and power the radio may use. **in force** is what the kernel applies now; if it differs from the configured one, the card decides by itself (newer Intel and Qualcomm keep the domain in firmware)."),
    ("iface_wifi.driver", "The kernel driver of the card (`rtw89_8852be` for a Realtek RTL8852BE, `iwlwifi` for Intel, `mt7921e` for MediaTek)."),
    ("iface_wifi.failure", "Why joining failed last: wrong passphrase, network not found, the access point refused or did not answer. The whole history is in Wi-Fi › History."),
    ("iface_wifi.firmware", "The firmware the card loaded, as the driver announced it in the kernel log. Useful to compare with the maker's fixes when the card misbehaves."),
    ("iface_wifi.last_heard", "How long since the card last heard the access point. Normally tens of milliseconds; seconds mean the link is about to drop."),
    ("iface_wifi.network", "The network name (SSID), the security negotiated (WPA2, WPA3), the cipher (CCMP is AES; GCMP-256 is WPA3-Enterprise 192-bit) and PMF (802.11w), which protects management frames against forced disconnection. WPA3 requires PMF."),
    ("iface_wifi.noise", "The channel's noise floor as the card measures it, and the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR = signal − noise). Above 25 dB is good; under 15 dB rates fall. Many drivers do not report noise."),
    ("iface_wifi.phy", "The physical radio as the kernel names it (`phy0`). A card may carry more than one interface on the same radio."),
    ("iface_wifi.retries", "Frames sent again because the access point did not acknowledge them the first time, as a share of what was sent. Up to ~10 % is normal; more means interference or a weak signal. **failed**: given up after every retry (real loss)."),
    ("iface_wifi.rx_rate", "The rate of the last frame received from the access point: speed, MCS (modulation and coding: higher is faster and needs a better signal), spatial streams, channel width and guard interval (GI). It changes all the time with the signal."),
    ("iface_wifi.signal", "How strong the access point arrives, in dBm (closer to zero is better). Wi-Fi design references: **excellent** from −55 dBm, **good** from −67 (the least for voice), **fair** to −75, **poor** below. **avg** averages its frames; **beacons**, its beacons."),
    ("iface_wifi.standards", "The 802.11 standards the card speaks across its bands: a/b/g (old), n (Wi-Fi 4), ac (Wi-Fi 5), ax (Wi-Fi 6), be (Wi-Fi 7)."),
    ("iface_wifi.state", "**connected**: on the network. **searching**: looking for it. **connecting**: authenticating or exchanging keys. **blocked**: the radio is off by a switch or key. **idle**: no network asked for. After the state: how long it has been connected and how many reconnects there were."),
    ("iface_wifi.switch", "The radio kill switch (rfkill): **soft** is software (airplane mode), **hard** is a physical switch or key. Blocked, the radio sends nothing until it is freed there."),
    ("iface_wifi.tx_power", "The transmit power in use and the most the country in force allows on this channel. The card adjusts it; the legal limit comes from the regulatory domain."),
    ("iface_wifi.tx_rate", "The rate of the last frame sent to the access point: speed, MCS, spatial streams, width and guard interval. Useful throughput is typically 50–70 % of it."),
];
